>	While reading this group I have come across a number of
>references to Mjollnir. What sort of weapon is this, and why is it so
>desirable? Also, does anyone have any suggestions on how to deal with
>cockatrices, which have claimed my four best characters so far?

SPOILER WARNING:


Mjollner does extra damage by shocking.  It still has a hit bonus of +5,
so you are more likely to hit your enemy with Mjollner than with a regular
war hammer.  But if the enemy id shock resistant, then you will only do
normal war hammer damage, which is not very exiting.

However, if your enemy does not have shock resistance, then Mjollner is easily
the most deadly of weapons.  Imagine wielding a +24 war hammer.  That is the
level of damage that the shock bonus adds to Mjollner.  Your plusses added
through enchantments are in addition to this.

For cockatrices, make sure you are wearing gloves of some kind before you
attack them.  If you start "feeling stiff", then eat either a lizard corpse,
which will cure you without damaging you, or eat the corpse of an acidic
creature, which will cure you and damage you.

If you kill the cockatrice, and you know where all the traps are on the level
(see spoiler that follows), then wield it and attack the monsters with it
(make sure you are wearing gloves).

Never go down stairs or fall through a trap door while wielding a cockatrice.
Never stumble (as in when wearing boots of fumbling) while wielding a
cockatrice.  This is a fatal mistake.

SPOILER FOLLOWS:
How to find all traps on a level - painlessly - this may seem obvious to some
of you, but it's not in the spoiler sheet, and I had to examine the source
code before I figured this out

Get yourself a crystal ball, and apply it.  When it asks you what you want
to look for, type "^".  This finds traps as if you had swallowed a new potion:
detect traps.

Note - don't bother if the crystal ball is cursed.

Crystal balls are great for finding stuff that has a symbol, like "%", etc.
